The deployment of the DNSGuard Enterprise platform presents distinct architectural limitations depending on whether it is hosted locally or within a serverless cloud environment like Vercel. When executed on a local system, the application benefits from full filesystem write permissions and access to native operating system libraries. This allows the web dashboard to persistently overwrite configuration files (such as rules.yaml) and enables the parsing of raw binary .pcap files utilizing underlying network drivers like libpcap. However, local deployment inherently restricts the platform's scalability, continuous uptime, and remote accessibility, binding its performance entirely to the host machine's hardware capacity.

Conversely, deploying the platform in a virtualized, serverless cloud environment ensures global accessibility and automatic scaling but introduces strict execution constraints. Serverless containers operate statelessly with a read-only filesystem, meaning any dynamic detection rule adjustments made via the web interface are immediately lost when the ephemeral execution container spins down. Overcoming this requires decoupling the configuration state from the local filesystem and migrating it to an external cloud database. Furthermore, the heavily sandboxed nature of cloud functions strips away the low-level network drivers required for raw packet analysis, limiting log ingestion strictly to pre-parsed, standard text formats such as CSV or JSON.
